Q: How do you strip quotes out of an ECHO&#39;ed string in a Windows batch file? A: <p>I needed to echo a string without quotes, but with the | character and with a new line. <strong>I tried many solutions and none of them worked</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>My solution works with the | character and also prints a new line.</strong></p>
<p>This is my test data:</p>
<pre><code>call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with quotes&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with nested &quot;quotes&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with more &quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with even more &quot;&quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with | quotes&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with | nested &quot;quotes&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with | more &quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with | even more &quot;&quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>This removes only outer quotes:</p>
<pre><code>:echo_no_quotes
    :: put argument in quotes first to allow | character
    set string=&quot;###%~1###&quot;
    :: substitute | character first
    set string=%string:|=^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^|%  

    :: remove outer quotes  
    SET string=%string:&quot;###=%
    SET string=%string:###&quot;=%
    SET string=%string:###=%

    :: colon to allow empty lines
    echo:%string%
EXIT /B 0
</code></pre>
<p>This is the output:</p>
<pre><code>test with quotes  
test with nested &quot;quotes&quot;  
test with more &quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;  
test with even more &quot;&quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;  
test with | quotes  
test with | nested &quot;quotes&quot;  
test with | more &quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;  
test with | even more &quot;&quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;  
</code></pre>
<p>to echo to a file instead substitute:</p>
<pre><code>echo:%string% &gt;&gt; test.txt
</code></pre>
<p>for:</p>
<pre><code>echo:%string%
</code></pre>
<p>EDIT:</p>
<p>Updated it with the ability to print more special characters:</p>
<pre><code>@ECHO OFF

call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with quotes&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with nested &quot;quotes&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with more &quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with even more &quot;&quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with |&lt;&gt;&amp;&quot;

:: these characters need special escaping:
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with ^^&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with %%%%&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with ^*&quot;
exit /B

:echo_no_quotes
    :: put argument in quotes first to allow | character
    set string=&quot;###%~1###&quot;
    :: substitute ^ character first
    set string=%string:^^=^^^^^^^^% 
    :: substitute other special characters:
    set string=%string:|=^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^|%  
    set string=%string:&lt;=^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^&lt;% 
    set string=%string:&gt;=^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^&gt;% 
    set string=%string:&amp;=^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^&amp;%  

    :: remove outer quotes  
    SET string=%string:&quot;###=%
    SET string=%string:###&quot;=%
    SET string=%string:###=%

    :: colon to allow empty lines
    echo:%string%
EXIT /B 0
</code></pre>
<p>EDIT2:
There is a way to use EnableDelayedExpansion. It doesn't set it globally for the script. That would have been an option too, but for some scripts that's undesirable. Thanks to jeb.</p>
<pre><code>@ECHO OFF
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with quotes&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with nested &quot;quotes&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with more &quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with even more &quot;&quot;&quot;quotes&quot;&quot;&quot;&quot;
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with `~!@#$^&amp;*()_+-=[]\{}|;':,.&lt;&gt;/?&quot;

:: these characters need special escaping:
call :echo_no_quotes &quot;test with %%%%&quot;

:echo_no_quotes
    set &quot;string=%~1&quot; &amp; setlocal EnableDelayedExpansion &amp; echo(!string:^^^^=^^!
EXIT /B 0
</code></pre>

Q: Count rows of tables identified by a subquery A: <p>This works for me in SQLite:</p>
<pre><code>select count(*) from (subquery);
</code></pre>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>select count(*) as differentFirstNames from (select distinct f_name from names);
</code></pre>
